# .NET build output (print-clients/windows) — these were previously
# committed by accident, leaving stale build artifacts (including a
# renamed exe under its old name) sitting in git indefinitely.
print-clients/windows/bin/
print-clients/windows/obj/

.vercel

# `.env*` has been here all along -- but .env was committed BEFORE the rule
# existed, and a tracked file ignores .gitignore entirely, so the rule looked
# like it was working while the file went on being published with every push.
# `git rm --cached .env` is what actually detaches it; the rule only keeps it
# detached. Everything in it is publishable (anon key, project URL, SP-API
# solution id), so nothing was exposed -- but a tracked .env is how the first
# real secret someone adds gets published out of habit.
#
# .env.example is the exception: a template with no values, so a fresh
# checkout knows what to fill in.
.env*
!.env.example
